// =============================================================================
// INVENTORY — where a thing IS, how much of it there is, and everything that
// ever moved it.
//
// `products.prisma` says what a thing IS and has no quantity anywhere in it.
// This file is the other half: every quantity in the programme lives here, and
// exactly one table is allowed to state one.
//
// ── PI-ADR-004: `stock_ledger` IS THE ONLY SOURCE OF QUANTITY TRUTH ──────────
//
//                  recordMovement()          <- the ONLY writer
//                         |
//                         v
//                  stock_ledger              append-only, two enforcement layers
//                         |  trigger
//                         v
//                  stock_balances            a CACHE. Never authoritative.
//
//   1. Nothing outside `recordMovement()` inserts into `stock_ledger`.
//   2. Nothing at all writes `stock_balances`. The trigger does, and `rcln_app`
//      holds no INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE on it to make that literal rather than
//      aspirational.
//   3. A correction is a COMPENSATING MOVEMENT with a reason, never an edit.
//   4. `stock_balances` disagreeing with a ledger replay is a bug in the
//      trigger, and the ledger wins. `verifyBalances()` replays and compares.
//   5. Quantities are stored in the product's BASE UNIT. What the user actually
//      typed is recorded beside it (`quantity_entered` + `unit_id`) so a receipt
//      of "2 boxes" still reads as "2 boxes" a year later.
//
// ⚠️ THE CALLER NEVER CHOOSES A SIGN. `movement_type` determines it and a CHECK
//   constraint enforces the pairing — see `stock_ledger_direction` in the
//   migration. A caller that COULD pass `-5` on a goods receipt is a caller that
//   eventually will, and the resulting row is indistinguishable from a genuine
//   issue in every report that reads the ledger.
//
// ── TENANCY: STRICTLY TENANT ROWS, NEVER PLATFORM (PI-ADR-003) ───────────────
// Nothing in this file allows a NULL `organization_id`, and nothing in it is
// platform-extensible. A location, a batch, a serial and a movement are facts
// about ONE clinic at ONE site. Every table here carries `organization_id` AND
// `branch_id`, both NOT NULL, and joins BOTH the `org_scoped` and the
// `branch_scoped` RLS arrays — the shape the invoice tables use, and for the
// same reason: a child that inherits only the organization half of a
// branch-scoped parent re-opens the branch boundary (see enable-rls.sql).
//
// ⚠️ EVERY FK POINTING AT A POSSIBLY-PLATFORM ROW NEEDS A RESTRICTIVE
//   `*_visible` POLICY, and this file has SEVEN of them. `batches.product_id`
//   CANNOT be a composite `(organization_id, product_id)` FK: a clinic
//   legitimately stocks a PLATFORM product, whose `organization_id` is NULL and
//   which a NOT NULL column cannot compose with. `tenant_isolation` therefore
//   constrains the batch's OWN organization and says nothing whatsoever about
//   the product it names. Without the policy a clinic creates a batch of another
//   clinic's private product and reads its name straight back out of the join.
//   This was the single most likely security regression in PI-1 and it is the
//   single most likely one here.
//
// ── QUANTITIES AND MONEY (PI-ADR-010) ───────────────────────────────────────
// `Decimal(18,6)` for quantity — base units include mL, mg and fractional
// tablets, and three places is not enough for a base unit of mg in a pack sold
// by the gram. Integer minor units for money, PER BASE UNIT, so a supplier
// changing their pack size does not invalidate the cost of what is on the shelf.
// Never a float, in either.
// =============================================================================

/// What a storage location IS, for grouping in the UI and for matching against a
/// product's storage requirements.
///
/// ⚠️ METADATA, NEVER AUTHORIZATION. Do not gate a permission on it and do not
///   infer a regulatory control from it: a `CONTROLLED_CABINET` is where a
///   clinic chose to put a cabinet, not a statement that the law requires one.
///   `requires_controlled_access` on the location and the jurisdiction rules in
///   PI-5 answer that. Same trap `ProductType` and `TaxonomyNodeType` already
///   carry a warning about.
enum LocationKind {
  MAIN_PHARMACY
  SATELLITE_PHARMACY
  REFRIGERATOR
  FREEZER
  CONTROLLED_CABINET
  DEPARTMENT_STORE
  PROCEDURE_ROOM
  LAB_STORE
  DENTAL_STORE
  VETERINARY_STORE
  CENTRAL_WAREHOUSE
  /// Stock owned by a supplier and held on the clinic's premises.
  CONSIGNMENT
  /// The quarantine bin things go to before they are destroyed.
  DISPOSAL
}

/// Where a BATCH is in its own lifecycle.
///
/// ⚠️ NOT A PRODUCT STATUS AND NOT A STOCK STATUS (PI-ADR-013). Three enums,
///   three columns, no shared vocabulary, and nothing may read one to infer
///   another. A `DISCONTINUED` product with an `ACTIVE` batch on the shelf is
///   normal and must stay dispensable; an `ACTIVE` product with a `RECALLED`
///   batch must not.
///
/// This is the batch's disposition as a whole. What is physically WHERE, and in
/// what condition, is `StockStatus` on the balance rows — one batch can have
/// some units quarantined at one location and some available at another, which
/// a single status on the batch cannot express.
enum BatchStatus {
  ACTIVE
  QUARANTINED
  EXPIRED
  RECALLED
  DAMAGED
  DISPOSED
}

/// The condition a QUANTITY is held in. The second axis of PI-ADR-013.
///
/// ⚠️ ONLY `AVAILABLE` IS ALLOCATABLE. Everything else is visible, countable and
///   valued but not dispensable — which is exactly why quarantine works at all.
///   A recall that made stock disappear would leave the clinic unable to say how
///   much it is about to send back.
enum StockStatus {
  AVAILABLE
  /// Spoken for by a pending dispense, an order or a scheduled procedure.
  /// Becomes real in PI-3; the status exists here so the ledger never needs a
  /// migration to record one.
  RESERVED
  QUARANTINED
  /// Held by a regulator, a quality hold, or a manual review.
  BLOCKED
  EXPIRED
  DAMAGED
  RECALLED
  DISPOSED
  /// Sent from one branch and not yet received at the other. PI-3.
  IN_TRANSIT
}

/// A serialised unit's own lifecycle. Devices and implants only.
enum SerialStatus {
  IN_STOCK
  /// Fitted to, or issued to, a patient. `assigned_patient_id` is then set.
  ISSUED
  RETURNED
  QUARANTINED
  RECALLED
  DAMAGED
  DISPOSED
}

/// Why a quantity moved.
///
/// ⚠️ THE SIGN IS A PROPERTY OF THE MEMBER, NOT A CHOICE OF THE CALLER. The
///   `stock_ledger_direction` CHECK in the migration pairs each member with the
///   sign it is allowed to carry AND with which of `status_from` / `status_to`
///   it must name. Three shapes:
///
///     ADDS      PURCHASE_RECEIPT, TRANSFER_IN, RETURN
///               quantity > 0, status_to only.
///     REMOVES   DISPENSING, CLINICAL_CONSUMPTION, TRANSFER_OUT, DISPOSAL,
///               PURCHASE_RETURN
///               quantity < 0, status_from only.
///     MOVES     RESERVATION, RELEASE, EXPIRY, RECALL, QUARANTINE,
///               QUARANTINE_RELEASE, DAMAGE
///               quantity > 0, BOTH statuses, and they must differ. Net effect
///               on the branch's total holding is zero — the quantity changes
///               which bucket it sits in, not whether it exists.
///     EITHER    ADJUSTMENT — and therefore the only member that REQUIRES a
///               reason code, because it is the only one whose meaning is not
///               already stated by its name.
///
/// ⚠️ `EXPIRY`, `DAMAGE` AND `RECALL` ARE MOVES, NOT REMOVALS, AND THAT IS A
///   DELIBERATE REFINEMENT OF THE ARCHITECTURE DOC'S SIGN TABLE. Expired stock
///   has not left the building; it is on the shelf, undispensable, waiting to be
///   destroyed and needing to be counted and valued until it is. Writing it as a
///   `−` would make it vanish from every count on the day it expired and leave
///   the clinic unable to say what it is about to dispose of — while the status
///   model in INVENTORY_ARCHITECTURE.md says in the same breath that EXPIRED
///   stock is "visible, countable and valued". `DISPOSAL` is the `−` that
///   actually removes it, and it is the one that reports a physical departure.
enum StockMovementType {
  PURCHASE_RECEIPT
  TRANSFER_IN
  TRANSFER_OUT
  DISPENSING
  CLINICAL_CONSUMPTION
  ADJUSTMENT
  DAMAGE
  EXPIRY
  RECALL
  /// Coming back from a patient or a department, INTO stock. Not a purchase
  /// return, which is `PURCHASE_RETURN` immediately below.
  RETURN
  /// Going back to the SUPPLIER (PI-4.7).
  ///
  /// ⚠️ ITS OWN MEMBER, AND A DELIBERATE REFINEMENT OF WHAT `RETURN`'S COMMENT
  ///   SAID IN PI-2 — which was that a purchase return would be a `TRANSFER_OUT`
  ///   to the supplier. It cannot be. `TRANSFER_OUT` means "went to another
  ///   branch of ours" to every report that reads this column, and the
  ///   outstanding-quantity arithmetic on `stock_transfers` reads exactly those
  ///   rows: a purchase return written as one would show up as stock in transit
  ///   between two of the clinic's own sites, on a van that does not exist.
  ///   Distinguishable only by `reference_type`, which no aggregate groups by.
  ///
  /// ⚠️ AND IT HAS NO DEFAULT `status_from`, FOR THE REASON `DISPOSAL` HAS NONE.
  ///   What is going back — sound stock ordered in error, stock rejected at
  ///   inspection, stock damaged in the box — is the entire content of the
  ///   record. See `DEFAULT_STATUS` in `packages/inventory/src/movement.ts`.
  PURCHASE_RETURN
  DISPOSAL
  RESERVATION
  RELEASE
  QUARANTINE
  QUARANTINE_RELEASE
}

/// What a movement was FOR. Half of the traceability key.
///
/// `reference_id` is deliberately NOT a foreign key: the row it names lives in a
/// different domain, several of those domains do not exist yet
/// (`prescriptions`, `encounters`, goods receipts), and a ledger row must
/// outlive whatever caused it. A recall in 2031 asks "what did this batch touch"
/// and must get an answer even if the encounter was purged under a retention
/// policy. The pair is indexed so the question is one index scan.
enum StockReferenceType {
  /// No external cause: an opening balance, a stock take, a manual correction.
  MANUAL
  GOODS_RECEIPT
  PURCHASE_RETURN
  DISPENSE
  CONSUMPTION
  TRANSFER
  STOCK_TAKE
  RECALL
  /// The expiry sweep. The only reference type written by a worker.
  EXPIRY_SWEEP
  ONLINE_ORDER
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Places
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A place stock is kept. `branch -> location -> area -> bin` (PI-ADR-012).
///
/// ⚠️ A BRANCH HAS MANY LOCATIONS, AND ASSUMING ONE IS UNRECOVERABLE WITHOUT A
///   MIGRATION UNDER LIVE STOCK. A clinic has a main pharmacy, a vaccine fridge,
///   a controlled-drug cabinet and a dressing trolley in every procedure room,
///   and those are four different answers to "where is it" with four different
///   storage requirements and four different people responsible. Folding them
///   into "the branch's stock" makes cold-chain, controlled-substance
///   reconciliation and departmental consumption all unexpressible at once.
///
/// Branch-scoped: `branch_id` is NOT NULL and the branch policy is therefore
/// absolute. A storekeeper scoped to one site cannot read another site's shelves.
model InventoryLocation {
  id                       String       @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId           String       @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid
  branchId                 String       @map("branch_id") @db.Uuid
  kind                     LocationKind
  /// The clinic's own code. Unique per branch, not per organization — two sites
  /// both calling their fridge `FRIDGE-1` is the ordinary case.
  code                     String       @db.VarChar(64)
  name                     String       @db.VarChar(255)
  /// Whether stock may be dispensed or consumed directly FROM here. A central
  /// warehouse usually cannot; a main pharmacy always can. UI and PI-7 read it;
  /// ⚠️ it is not a permission.
  isDispensingPoint        Boolean      @default(false) @map("is_dispensing_point")
  /// A locked cabinet with a register. Descriptive here; PI-5 decides which
  /// products a jurisdiction REQUIRES it for.
  requiresControlledAccess Boolean      @default(false) @map("requires_controlled_access")
  /// What this place can physically keep — the fridge's own range. Matched
  /// against a product's `storage_profile_id` when stock is received, so a
  /// vaccine into an ambient shelf can be refused. A plain FK into a
  /// possibly-platform row, so it carries a RESTRICTIVE visibility policy.
  storageProfileId         String?      @map("storage_profile_id") @db.Uuid
  isActive                 Boolean      @default(true) @map("is_active")
  createdAt                DateTime     @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt                DateTime     @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  deletedAt                DateTime?    @map("deleted_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization   Organization               @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  branch         Branch                     @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  storageProfile StorageRequirementProfile? @relation(fields: [storageProfileId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  areas                   StorageArea[]
  serialsHere             Serial[]
  balances                StockBalance[]
  movementsFrom           StockLedgerEntry[] @relation("MovementFromLocation")
  movementsTo             StockLedgerEntry[] @relation("MovementToLocation")
  // PI-3. Both sides of a transfer and everything reserved on this shelf.
  transfersFrom           StockTransfer[]    @relation("TransferFromLocation")
  transfersTo             StockTransfer[]    @relation("TransferToLocation")
  reservations            StockReservation[]
  // PI-4. Where a delivery lands, where a return leaves from, and where a
  // purchase order asked for it to be put.
  purchaseOrderDeliveries PurchaseOrder[]    @relation("PurchaseOrderDeliverTo")
  goodsReceipts           GoodsReceipt[]     @relation("GoodsReceiptLocation")
  purchaseReturns         PurchaseReturn[]   @relation("PurchaseReturnLocation")
  // PI-7. The counter a supply went out of, the shelf each allocated lot came
  // off, and where a return was put back.
  dispenses               Dispense[]           @relation("DispenseLocation")
  dispenseAllocations     DispenseAllocation[] @relation("DispenseAllocationLocation")
  dispenseReturns         DispenseReturn[]     @relation("DispenseReturnLocation")
  // PI-9. The trolley, cabinet or treatment room a procedure's material came
  // off. ⚠️ NOT required to be a dispensing point — see `ClinicalConsumption`.
  consumptions            ClinicalConsumption[]   @relation("ConsumptionLocation")
  consumptionAllocations  ConsumptionAllocation[] @relation("ConsumptionAllocationLocation")

  @@unique([organizationId, branchId, code])
  /// The composite-FK target every child below references.
  @@unique([organizationId, id])
  /// ⚠️ THE BRANCH-QUALIFIED TARGET, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES A CROSS-BRANCH
  ///   MOVEMENT UNREPRESENTABLE RATHER THAN MERELY REFUSED. With only
  ///   `(organization_id, id)` to compose against, `stock_ledger.branch_id` and
  ///   `from_location_id` are constrained independently — so a row at branch A
  ///   citing a shelf at branch B is a legal INSERT, and the only thing standing
  ///   there is a check in `recordMovementIn`. ADR-0004 argues the opposite
  ///   everywhere else in this schema; this is that argument applied here.
  @@unique([organizationId, branchId, id])
  @@index([organizationId, branchId, isActive])
  @@index([organizationId, branchId, kind])
  @@map("inventory_locations")
}

/// A named part of a location — an aisle, a shelf, a fridge drawer.
///
/// Areas and bins are NOT separately branch-scoped (PI-ADR-012): they inherit
/// through the composite FK, exactly as invoice children do. They carry
/// `branch_id` anyway so they are ordinary members of both RLS loops rather than
/// needing a hand-written parent predicate — see the note against
/// `appointment_status_history` in enable-rls.sql for what happens otherwise.
model StorageArea {
  id             String   @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId String   @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid
  branchId       String   @map("branch_id") @db.Uuid
  locationId     String   @map("location_id") @db.Uuid
  code           String   @db.VarChar(64)
  name           String   @db.VarChar(255)
  isActive       Boolean  @default(true) @map("is_active")
  createdAt      DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt      DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization Organization      @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  branch       Branch            @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  location     InventoryLocation @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId, locationId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  bins         StorageBin[]

  @@unique([organizationId, locationId, code])
  @@unique([organizationId, id])
  @@index([organizationId, branchId, locationId])
  @@map("storage_areas")
}

/// The smallest addressable place. A shelf position, a tote, a drawer slot.
///
/// Bins are addresses, not quantities: `stock_balances` is keyed by LOCATION,
/// not by bin, because a bin is where a person walks to and a location is what
/// the clinic reconciles. Bin-level balances would multiply the balance table by
/// the number of shelves for a number nobody counts.
model StorageBin {
  id             String   @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId String   @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid
  branchId       String   @map("branch_id") @db.Uuid
  areaId         String   @map("area_id") @db.Uuid
  code           String   @db.VarChar(64)
  label          String?  @db.VarChar(255)
  isActive       Boolean  @default(true) @map("is_active")
  createdAt      DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt      DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  branch       Branch       @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  area         StorageArea  @relation(fields: [organizationId, areaId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([organizationId, areaId, code])
  /// ⚠️ PRESENT SO PI-3 CAN COMPOSITE-FK TO A BIN WITHOUT A MIGRATION UNDER LIVE
  ///   STOCK. Every other table in this file that anything might point at has
  ///   one; bins did not, purely because nothing points at them today. Bin-level
  ///   movements are exactly the kind of thing a later phase adds.
  @@unique([organizationId, id])
  @@index([organizationId, branchId, areaId])
  @@map("storage_bins")
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What is being counted
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A lot of one product, received once, with one expiry and one cost.
///
/// ⚠️ LOT UNIQUENESS IS TENANT- AND BRANCH-QUALIFIED, NEVER GLOBAL. A lot number
///   is the MANUFACTURER's identifier, so two clinics stocking the same medicine
///   hold the same lot number, and one hospital group receiving that lot at two
///   sites holds it twice — at two costs, with two remaining quantities. A bare
///   `@@unique([lotNumber])` would make the second receipt fail with a duplicate
///   key nobody could explain.
///
/// ⚠️ `product_id` IS A PLAIN FK AND CANNOT BE COMPOSITE. A clinic stocks
///   PLATFORM products, whose `organization_id` is NULL and which this row's NOT
///   NULL column cannot compose with. The RESTRICTIVE `product_visible` policy
///   is the ENTIRE control on that side — see the file header.
///
/// The manufacturer here may legitimately differ from the product's: contract
/// manufacture and repackaging are why the column exists rather than being read
/// off the product.
model Batch {
  id             String      @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId String      @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid
  branchId       String      @map("branch_id") @db.Uuid
  productId      String      @map("product_id") @db.Uuid
  /// As printed on the pack. Case is preserved; comparison is exact, because a
  /// lot number is an identifier and `AB12` is not `ab12` to a regulator.
  lotNumber      String      @map("lot_number") @db.VarChar(128)
  /// DATES, not instants. A batch is manufactured and expires on a DAY, in the
  /// manufacturer's calendar, in nobody's particular timezone. The sweep that
  /// acts on `expires_on` is what resolves it into the BRANCH's zone — see
  /// invariant 6 and the expiry processor.
  manufacturedOn DateTime?   @map("manufactured_on") @db.Date
  expiresOn      DateTime?   @map("expires_on") @db.Date
  /// Some products are re-tested and re-dated rather than expiring outright —
  /// reagents and some APIs. Informational until PI-5 gives it a rule.
  retestOn       DateTime?   @map("retest_on") @db.Date
  manufacturerId String?     @map("manufacturer_id") @db.Uuid
  /// Cost per BASE UNIT in integer minor units (PI-ADR-010). Per base unit, not
  /// per pack: a supplier changing from strips of 10 to strips of 15 must not
  /// silently restate the value of everything already on the shelf.
  unitCostBase   BigInt?     @map("unit_cost_base") @db.BigInt
  /// ISO 4217. Required whenever a cost is recorded — CHECKed in the migration,
  /// because a number with no currency is a number that will be added to a
  /// different one.
  currency       String?     @db.Char(3)
  status         BatchStatus @default(ACTIVE)

  // -- recall and quarantine capability (PI-2.9) ------------------------------
  // The COLUMNS land here; the workflow is PI-10. That ordering is deliberate:
  // a recall the schema cannot represent is a migration under load, and recalls
  // arrive without notice. From today, allocation excludes RECALLED and
  // QUARANTINED stock, so a batch set to either by hand is immediately
  // un-dispensable even though no workflow exists yet.
  quarantinedAt    DateTime? @map("quarantined_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  quarantineReason String?   @map("quarantine_reason") @db.Text
  recalledAt       DateTime? @map("recalled_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// The regulator's or manufacturer's own notice number, so a batch can be tied
  /// back to the announcement that caused it.
  recallReference  String?   @map("recall_reference") @db.VarChar(128)

  /// When this lot physically arrived. The FEFO tie-break: two batches expiring
  /// the same day go out oldest-received first.
  receivedAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("received_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  notes      String?  @db.Text
  createdAt  DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt  DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization Organization  @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  branch       Branch        @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// Plain FKs into possibly-platform rows. See the file header — each carries a
  /// RESTRICTIVE `*_visible` policy, which is the only control on that side.
  product      Product       @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  manufacturer Manufacturer? @relation(fields: [manufacturerId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  serials               Serial[]
  balances              StockBalance[]
  movements             StockLedgerEntry[]
  /// PI-3. A lot appears on a transfer line as the SOURCE at the sending branch
  /// and as the DESTINATION at the receiving one — two rows, two branches, one
  /// manufacturer's lot number. See `StockTransferLine`.
  transferLinesSent     StockTransferLine[]  @relation("TransferLineSourceBatch")
  transferLinesReceived StockTransferLine[]  @relation("TransferLineDestinationBatch")
  reservations          StockReservation[]
  /// PI-4. The delivery that created this lot, and any of it that went back.
  goodsReceiptLines     GoodsReceiptLine[]   @relation("GoodsReceiptLineBatch")
  purchaseReturnLines   PurchaseReturnLine[] @relation("PurchaseReturnLineBatch")
  /// PI-7. ⚠️ THE RECALL PATH. "Which supplies came out of this lot" is the
  /// question a recall asks first, and this relation plus
  /// `dispense_allocations(organization_id, batch_id)` is how it is answered.
  dispenseAllocations   DispenseAllocation[] @relation("DispenseAllocationBatch")
  /// PI-9. The other half of the same recall question: a lot consumed during a
  /// procedure never went out of a counter, so a recall that walked only
  /// `dispense_allocations` would miss every implant ever fitted.
  consumptionAllocations ConsumptionAllocation[] @relation("ConsumptionAllocationBatch")

  @@unique([organizationId, branchId, productId, lotNumber])
  @@unique([organizationId, id])
  /// Branch-qualified, for the same reason as `inventory_locations`. A movement
  /// at branch A citing a lot held at branch B is now unrepresentable.
  @@unique([organizationId, branchId, id])
  /// FEFO allocation and the expiry sweep, which is the same query twice.
  @@index([organizationId, branchId, productId, expiresOn])
  @@index([organizationId, branchId, expiresOn])
  @@index([organizationId, productId])
  @@map("batches")
}

/// One physically identifiable unit. An implant, a device, an instrument set.
///
/// ⚠️ `assigned_patient_id` IS PHI. "Serial 7742 is in Mrs Rao" is a clinical
///   fact about a named person, answerable by primary key, and it is why this
///   table is branch-scoped rather than org-scoped: a device fitted at one site
///   is not the other site's business. Every read that returns it goes through
///   `recordDataAccess` (PI-ADR-016), and the id is never logged beside a name.
///
/// A serial may exist without a batch: a device with `tracking_mode = SERIAL`
/// and no lot is ordinary. `LOT_AND_SERIAL` requires both, and the ledger's
/// tracking CHECK is what enforces that at the point it matters.
model Serial {
  id                String       @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId    String       @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid
  branchId          String       @map("branch_id") @db.Uuid
  productId         String       @map("product_id") @db.Uuid
  batchId           String?      @map("batch_id") @db.Uuid
  serialNumber      String       @map("serial_number") @db.VarChar(128)
  status            SerialStatus @default(IN_STOCK)
  /// Where it is right now. NULL once it has left the clinic.
  currentLocationId String?      @map("current_location_id") @db.Uuid
  /// ⚠️ PHI. See the model comment.
  assignedPatientId String?      @map("assigned_patient_id") @db.Uuid
  assignedAt        DateTime?    @map("assigned_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// A device's own expiry, where it has one distinct from its batch's.
  expiresOn         DateTime?    @map("expires_on") @db.Date
  notes             String?      @db.Text
  createdAt         DateTime     @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt         DateTime     @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization    Organization       @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  branch          Branch             @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// Plain FK into a possibly-platform row — `product_visible` policy.
  product         Product            @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  /// Branch-qualified: a serial held at one site cannot cite a lot or a shelf at
  /// another. `assertSerialReferences` says so in a sentence; this says so in
  /// the schema.
  batch           Batch?             @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId, batchId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  currentLocation InventoryLocation? @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId, currentLocationId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  assignedPatient Patient?           @relation(fields: [organizationId, assignedPatientId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Restrict)

  balances            StockBalance[]
  movements           StockLedgerEntry[]
  /// PI-3. ⚠️ A serial crossing a branch boundary MOVES — `branch_id` is updated
  /// at receipt rather than a second row being created, because a serial IS the
  /// physical device and two rows would be two devices. That update is the one
  /// place in the transfer flow that needs a caller scoped to both branches; see
  /// `receiveTransfer`.
  transferLines       StockTransferLine[]  @relation("TransferLineSerial")
  reservations        StockReservation[]
  /// PI-4. The delivery that first recorded this device, and its return.
  goodsReceiptLines   GoodsReceiptLine[]   @relation("GoodsReceiptLineSerial")
  purchaseReturnLines PurchaseReturnLine[] @relation("PurchaseReturnLineSerial")
  /// PI-7. The supply that issued this device to somebody.
  dispenseAllocations DispenseAllocation[] @relation("DispenseAllocationSerial")
  /// PI-9. The procedure that FITTED this device. ⚠️ This is what makes a device
  /// recall answerable at patient level, and it is why a consumed serial sets
  /// `assigned_patient_id` in the same transaction.
  consumptionAllocations ConsumptionAllocation[] @relation("ConsumptionAllocationSerial")

  /// Serial numbers repeat across manufacturers, so the key is product-qualified
  /// as well as tenant-qualified.
  @@unique([organizationId, productId, serialNumber])
  @@unique([organizationId, id])
  /// Branch-qualified, for the same reason as `inventory_locations`.
  @@unique([organizationId, branchId, id])
  @@index([organizationId, branchId, status])
  @@index([organizationId, batchId])
  /// "Which patient has device X" and "which devices does this patient have" —
  /// the recall question, from both ends.
  @@index([organizationId, assignedPatientId])
  @@map("serials")
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The ledger
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// EVERY movement of every quantity, for ever. The most important table in the
/// programme (PI-ADR-004).
///
/// ── APPEND-ONLY, ENFORCED TWICE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
/// Exactly as `audit_logs` and `data_access_logs` already are in this repository:
///
///   1. `rcln_app` holds no UPDATE or DELETE on the table (a REVOKE in the
///      migration), and
///   2. a trigger refuses both anyway, so re-running the init script's blanket
///      `GRANT ... ON ALL TABLES` does not quietly re-open it.
///
/// Each layer is measured by a test that removes the other and proves this one
/// still refuses. A correction is a compensating row with a reason; there is no
/// edit, and that is what makes recall and traceability possible at all.
///
/// ── WHY `reference_id` IS NOT A FOREIGN KEY ──────────────────────────────────
/// It points into whichever domain caused the movement, several of which do not
/// exist yet, and a ledger row must outlive its cause. See `StockReferenceType`.
///
/// ── THE DENORMALISED COLUMNS ARE DELIBERATE ─────────────────────────────────
/// `tracking_mode` is copied from the product so the PI-ADR-014 CHECK needs no
/// join — a CHECK constraint cannot read another table, and a rule that lives
/// only in a service is a rule until somebody writes a second service. It is a
/// snapshot of what the product required AT THE TIME, which is also the honest
/// thing for history to record: a product that becomes serialised next year did
/// not require a serial for a movement made today.
model StockLedgerEntry {
  id             String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId String @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid
  branchId       String @map("branch_id") @db.Uuid

  productId    String       @map("product_id") @db.Uuid
  batchId      String?      @map("batch_id") @db.Uuid
  serialId     String?      @map("serial_id") @db.Uuid
  /// Snapshot of `products.tracking_mode`. See the model comment.
  trackingMode TrackingMode @map("tracking_mode")

  movementType StockMovementType @map("movement_type")
  /// SIGNED, in the product's base unit. ⚠️ The sign is derived from
  /// `movementType` and CHECKed; no caller ever chooses it. See the enum.
  quantityBase Decimal           @map("quantity_base") @db.Decimal(18, 6)

  /// What the user actually typed, and in what. Kept so a receipt of "2 boxes"
  /// still reads as "2 boxes" a year later, when the pack size has changed and
  /// `quantity_base` alone can no longer be turned back into what was meant.
  quantityEntered Decimal @map("quantity_entered") @db.Decimal(18, 6)
  unitId          String  @map("unit_id") @db.Uuid

  /// Which bucket the quantity left and which it entered. Both, one, or the
  /// other, paired with `movementType` by the direction CHECK. The balance
  /// trigger reads exactly these four columns and nothing else.
  fromLocationId String?      @map("from_location_id") @db.Uuid
  toLocationId   String?      @map("to_location_id") @db.Uuid
  statusFrom     StockStatus? @map("status_from")
  statusTo       StockStatus? @map("status_to")

  /// Mandatory on `ADJUSTMENT` (CHECKed) and optional elsewhere. An adjustment
  /// with no reason is a number nobody can audit, and adjustments are precisely
  /// where shrinkage hides.
  reasonCode String? @map("reason_code") @db.VarChar(64)
  reasonNote String? @map("reason_note") @db.Text

  referenceType StockReferenceType @map("reference_type")
  /// The id in the referenced domain. Not a FK — see the model comment.
  referenceId   String?            @map("reference_id") @db.Uuid

  /// Cost carried on the movement itself, so a valuation report never has to
  /// guess what the batch cost when this row was written.
  unitCostBase BigInt? @map("unit_cost_base") @db.BigInt
  currency     String? @db.Char(3)

  /// Who did it. NOT NULL: a movement with no actor is a movement nobody can be
  /// asked about, and the expiry sweep supplies a real user rather than a
  /// fiction — see the processor.
  actorUserId String   @map("actor_user_id") @db.Uuid
  /// When it HAPPENED, which is not always when it was recorded: a receipt
  /// entered on Monday for goods that arrived on Friday is ordinary.
  occurredAt  DateTime @default(now()) @map("occurred_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// When the row was written. Immutable, and the two together are what make a
  /// backdated entry visible as one.
  recordedAt  DateTime @default(now()) @map("recorded_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization Organization       @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  branch       Branch             @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// Plain FK into a possibly-platform row — `product_visible` policy.
  product      Product            @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  unit         UnitOfMeasure      @relation(fields: [unitId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  /**
   * ⚠️ ALL FOUR COMPOSE ON `(organization_id, branch_id, …)`, NOT JUST THE
   * ORGANIZATION. The two-column form proves only that the lot, serial or
   * shelf belongs to this TENANT; a movement recorded at branch A citing a
   * shelf at branch B would satisfy it. `recordMovementIn` checks that in
   * code, and a rule enforced in one code path is a rule until somebody writes
   * a second one — which PI-3's transfers, PI-4's receipts and PI-7's
   * dispensing all are. Three columns makes it unrepresentable.
   */
  batch        Batch?             @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId, batchId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  serial       Serial?            @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId, serialId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  fromLocation InventoryLocation? @relation("MovementFromLocation", fields: [organizationId, branchId, fromLocationId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  toLocation   InventoryLocation? @relation("MovementToLocation", fields: [organizationId, branchId, toLocationId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  actor        User               @relation(fields: [actorUserId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// The ledger screen: one product's history at one branch, newest first.
  @@index([organizationId, branchId, productId, occurredAt(sort: Desc)])
  /// One batch's whole life. The recall query.
  @@index([organizationId, batchId, occurredAt])
  /// Traceability: "what did this goods receipt / this dispense do".
  @@index([organizationId, referenceType, referenceId])
  /// One device's history, end to end.
  @@index([organizationId, serialId])
  /// The replay `verifyBalances()` runs, and the branch-wide ledger view.
  @@index([organizationId, branchId, occurredAt(sort: Desc)])
  @@map("stock_ledger")
}

/// How much of what is where, in what condition. A CACHE (PI-ADR-004).
///
/// ⚠️ NEVER AUTHORITATIVE, AND NOTHING IN THE APPLICATION WRITES IT. `rcln_app`
///   holds no INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE here; the row is maintained by a
///   SECURITY DEFINER trigger on `stock_ledger` and by nothing else. That is the
///   difference between "we agreed not to" and "it cannot".
///
///   A disagreement between this table and a ledger replay is a bug in the
///   trigger, and the LEDGER WINS. `verifyBalances()` performs the replay and is
///   run by the test suite and on demand; it never silently repairs, because a
///   cache that heals itself hides the bug that broke it.
///
/// ⚠️ `quantity >= 0` IS A DATABASE CHECK, NOT A SERVICE RULE. It is the last
///   line against a dispense that beat another dispense to the same last strip:
///   the losing transaction fails on the constraint rather than committing a
///   negative shelf. See the concurrency note in INVENTORY_ARCHITECTURE.md — the
///   service takes `FOR UPDATE` on these rows first so the normal outcome is a
///   409 rather than a constraint error, and this is what happens when it does
///   not.
model StockBalance {
  id             String      @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId String      @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid
  branchId       String      @map("branch_id") @db.Uuid
  productId      String      @map("product_id") @db.Uuid
  batchId        String?     @map("batch_id") @db.Uuid
  serialId       String?     @map("serial_id") @db.Uuid
  locationId     String      @map("location_id") @db.Uuid
  status         StockStatus
  quantity       Decimal     @db.Decimal(18, 6)
  updatedAt      DateTime    @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization Organization      @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  branch       Branch            @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// Plain FK into a possibly-platform row — `product_visible` policy.
  product      Product           @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  /// Branch-qualified, exactly as on the ledger — and here it also makes
  /// `verifyBalances()` airtight: the replay groups without `branch_id`, which
  /// is only correct because a location now implies exactly one branch.
  batch        Batch?            @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId, batchId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  serial       Serial?           @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId, serialId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  location     InventoryLocation @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId, locationId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// The bucket key. ⚠️ Rewritten NULLS NOT DISTINCT in the migration:
  /// `batch_id` and `serial_id` are nullable, and under Postgres's default every
  /// untracked product's balance row would be unique against every other — the
  /// trigger's `ON CONFLICT` would never fire and each movement would insert a
  /// new row instead of accumulating.
  @@unique([organizationId, branchId, productId, batchId, serialId, locationId, status])
  /// "What is on this shelf" — the stock-by-location screen.
  @@index([organizationId, branchId, locationId, productId, status])
  /// "Where is this product" — across every location the caller can see.
  @@index([organizationId, productId, status])
  @@index([organizationId, batchId])
  @@map("stock_balances")
}

// =============================================================================
// PI-3 — MOVEMENTS. Everything below turns "a quantity moved" into "a quantity
// moved FOR A REASON, ON A DOCUMENT, THAT SOMEBODY SIGNED FOR".
//
// PI-2 gave the ledger one manual write path: `POST /v1/stock/movements`, an
// adjustment with a free-text reason string. That is enough to correct a count
// and not enough to run a store. This phase adds the three shapes a store
// actually needs, and every one of them writes through `recordMovementIn` and
// nothing else — the tables here hold the DOCUMENT, never the quantity.
//
//   stock_reason_codes   the controlled vocabulary an adjustment must cite
//   stock_transfers      stock leaving one place and arriving at another
//   stock_reservations   stock spoken for, and not yet gone
//
// ⚠️ NOT ONE OF THESE TABLES IS A SECOND SOURCE OF QUANTITY TRUTH, AND THE ONE
//   THAT LOOKS LIKE IT IS THE ONE TO WATCH. `stock_transfer_lines.quantity_base`
//   is what the document SAYS was sent; `stock_balances` is what the shelves
//   HOLD. They answer different questions and the second is the one that is
//   authoritative about stock — the line quantity is authoritative only about
//   the paperwork. Every screen that reads a line quantity is reading the
//   paperwork, and PI-ADR-004 rule 4 still says the ledger wins.
// =============================================================================

/// Which direction of adjustment a reason code may be cited on.
///
/// "Found in the fridge" cannot explain stock going missing and "broken in
/// transit" cannot explain it appearing, so a single flat list would let every
/// report aggregate increases and decreases under one heading that means neither.
enum StockReasonDirection {
  INCREASE
  DECREASE
  /// Legitimately both — a stock take corrects in whichever direction the count
  /// came out.
  BOTH
}

/// Where a transfer has got to.
///
/// ⚠️ THERE IS NO `IN_TRANSIT` STOCK STATUS IN THIS FLOW, AND THAT IS THE PI-3
///   DECISION. In-transit quantity is `sent_quantity_base − received_quantity_
///   base` on the LINES of a transfer in `DISPATCHED`, not a bucket in
///   `stock_balances`. See the model comment for why, at length.
enum StockTransferStatus {
  /// Being built. Nothing has moved and no ledger row exists.
  DRAFT
  /// Sent. The `TRANSFER_OUT` legs are written and the sending branch's shelves
  /// are already lighter. Nothing has arrived.
  DISPATCHED
  /// Some lines received, some outstanding. Still reachable from the receiving
  /// branch's "to receive" list, which is the whole point of the state.
  PARTIALLY_RECEIVED
  RECEIVED
  /// Killed before dispatch (no ledger rows) or after (a compensating
  /// `TRANSFER_IN` back to the sender). See `cancelTransfer`.
  CANCELLED
}

/// What a reservation is doing now.
enum StockReservationStatus {
  /// Quantity is sitting in the `RESERVED` bucket, held for this reference.
  ACTIVE
  /// Given back to `AVAILABLE` by a person.
  RELEASED
  /// Given back to `AVAILABLE` by the sweep, because `expires_at` passed.
  EXPIRED
  /// Dispensed or consumed. PI-7 and PI-9 move it out of `RESERVED` directly.
  CONSUMED
}

/// The controlled vocabulary an adjustment must cite (PI-3.1).
///
/// ⚠️ THE LEDGER STORES THE CODE AS A STRING AND NOT A FOREIGN KEY, DELIBERATELY.
///   `stock_ledger.reason_code` is `VARCHAR(64)` and stays that way. A ledger row
///   must outlive whatever explained it — exactly the reasoning already written
///   against `reference_id` — and a clinic that retires `EXPIRED_ON_SHELF` in
///   2029 must not thereby make every 2027 adjustment unreadable or, worse,
///   undeletable. The master constrains what may be WRITTEN; the string is what
///   is READ back, for ever.
///
/// ⚠️ PLATFORM-EXTENSIBLE (`organization_id` NULLABLE), WHICH IS A DIFFERENT CALL
///   FROM EVERY OTHER TABLE IN THIS FILE. Locations, lots and movements are facts
///   about one clinic (PI-ADR-003) and are strictly tenant rows. A reason code is
///   not a fact about a clinic, it is a WORD — "damaged", "expired", "counted
///   short" — and every clinic on earth needs the same dozen on the day it opens.
///   Shipping them as platform rows means a clinic can record its first
///   adjustment without first inventing a vocabulary, and a clinic that wants
///   `BROKEN_BY_LOCUM` adds its own. Same shape as `manufacturers` and
///   `product_categories` in PI-1, and it carries the same
///   `platform_rows_immutable` trigger and a RESTRICTIVE `*_visible` policy.
///
/// This is NOT a taxonomy and must not become one. No parent, no hierarchy. A
/// reason code is aggregated in a report; anything deeper belongs in the note.
model StockReasonCode {
  id             String  @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  /// NULL = platform row, visible to every tenant.
  organizationId String? @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid

  /// Uppercase, flat, and matched exactly against what a caller sends. Aggregated
  /// in reports, so free text would produce "Damaged", "damaged" and "DAMAGED "
  /// as three categories — the same reasoning as `reasonCode` in the contracts.
  code  String @db.VarChar(64)
  /// What a human picks from a list. Sentence case, and the only thing a screen
  /// shows.
  label String @db.VarChar(200)

  direction StockReasonDirection @default(BOTH)

  /// Whether citing this code forces a free-text note as well.
  ///
  /// `OTHER` is the code this exists for: a reason code that explains nothing
  /// unless somebody writes what actually happened. Enforced in the service, on
  /// the code that is cited, because a CHECK on `stock_ledger` cannot join to the
  /// master — the same reason `tracking_mode` is denormalised onto the ledger.
  requiresNote Boolean @default(false) @map("requires_note")

  /// Retired rather than deleted. A code cited by ten thousand ledger rows is a
  /// code that must keep rendering; `is_active = false` stops it being OFFERED.
  isActive  Boolean @default(true) @map("is_active")
  /// Presentation order in the picker. Ties break on `label`.
  sortOrder Int     @default(100) @map("sort_order") @db.SmallInt

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization Organization? @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// ⚠️ TENANT-QUALIFIED, NEVER A BARE `@@unique([code])`. A clinic naming its own
  ///   code `DAMAGED` must not collide with the platform's, and two clinics must
  ///   not collide with each other. The platform's own rows are unique among
  ///   themselves because `organization_id` is NULL for all of them and the index
  ///   is rewritten NULLS NOT DISTINCT in the migration — without that, every
  ///   platform code could be inserted twice.
  @@unique([organizationId, code])
  @@index([organizationId, isActive, sortOrder])
  @@map("stock_reason_codes")
}

/// Stock moving from one place to another, as a DOCUMENT (PI-3.2, PI-3.3).
///
/// ── WHY THE DOCUMENT HOLDS THE IN-TRANSIT QUANTITY ──────────────────────────
///
///     branch A                                        branch B
///       AVAILABLE ──TRANSFER_OUT──▶ (this document) ──TRANSFER_IN──▶ AVAILABLE
///                  actor scoped to A                actor scoped to B
///
/// INVENTORY_ARCHITECTURE.md says in-transit stock is held in an `IN_TRANSIT`
/// STATUS owned by the SENDING branch. ⚠️ THIS IS A DELIBERATE REFINEMENT OF
/// THAT, decided in PI-3, and it is forced by the same isolation guarantee that
/// makes the rest of this file safe.
///
/// `branch_isolation` is RESTRICTIVE on `stock_ledger` and `stock_balances`, so
/// every row written must carry a `branch_id` inside the writer's scope. An
/// in-transit bucket owned by branch A means the person RECEIVING at branch B
/// has to write a removal against branch A's bucket — a branch they cannot see,
/// and must not be able to see. The only ways to make that work are to widen the
/// receiving user's tenant context or to write the row twice, and the first
/// punches the first hole in the branch boundary while the second reintroduces
/// the second writer PI-ADR-004 exists to forbid.
///
/// So: neither branch holds in-transit stock in a bucket. THE DOCUMENT HOLDS IT.
/// Dispatch writes `TRANSFER_OUT` at the sender and nothing else; receipt writes
/// `TRANSFER_IN` at the receiver and nothing else; both legs cite this row's id
/// as `reference_id` with `reference_type = TRANSFER`. Each leg is written by
/// somebody scoped to exactly the branch it touches, and no context is ever
/// widened.
///
/// What is outstanding is `sent_quantity_base − received_quantity_base` summed
/// over the lines of every `DISPATCHED` transfer, which is a better answer than
/// a balance bucket was ever going to give: "3 boxes are missing" becomes
/// "transfer TRF-000042, sent by Anand from Central Store on the 4th, 3 boxes
/// outstanding". "It vanished between branches" is the single most common
/// real-world inventory complaint and this is the shape that answers it.
///
/// ⚠️ THE ONE COST, WRITTEN DOWN SO PI-22 DOES NOT REDISCOVER IT: in-transit
///   stock is NOT in `stock_balances`. A valuation report that sums that table
///   and stops is under-counting by whatever is on a van. It must add the
///   outstanding lines of `DISPATCHED` transfers. `verifyBalances()` is
///   unaffected — it replays the ledger, and both legs are ledger rows.
///
/// ── INTRA-BRANCH IS THE SAME TABLE AND NOT THE SAME FLOW ────────────────────
/// When `from_branch_id = to_branch_id` there is nothing to be in transit
/// between: `dispatchTransfer` writes both legs in ONE transaction and the
/// document goes straight to `RECEIVED`. It is still a document, because "who
/// moved the vaccines out of the fridge" is a question with an answer.
///
/// ── TENANCY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
/// ⚠️ TWO BRANCH COLUMNS AND THEREFORE NO `branch_id`, SO THIS TABLE IS **NOT**
///   IN THE `branch_scoped` RLS ARRAY. It gets a bespoke RESTRICTIVE policy —
///   `from_branch_id = ANY(scope) OR to_branch_id = ANY(scope)` — because a
///   transfer is legitimately the business of both ends and of neither third
///   party. Putting it in the array would have silently matched nothing (the
///   loop's predicate names a column this table does not have) and Postgres
///   would have raised at migration time; the bespoke policy is in
///   enable-rls.sql beside `appointment_status_history`, which is there for the
///   same "the generic loop does not fit" reason.
model StockTransfer {
  id             String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId String @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid

  /// Human-facing, issued from `number_sequences` on dispatch — never on create,
  /// because a draft that is abandoned must not burn a number in a series a
  /// storekeeper reads as a sequence.
  transferNumber String? @map("transfer_number") @db.VarChar(64)

  fromBranchId   String  @map("from_branch_id") @db.Uuid
  toBranchId     String  @map("to_branch_id") @db.Uuid
  fromLocationId String  @map("from_location_id") @db.Uuid
  /// Where it lands. Nullable until receipt: the sender proposes a shelf and the
  /// RECEIVER decides, because the sender does not know which fridge has room.
  toLocationId   String? @map("to_location_id") @db.Uuid

  /// ⚠️ THE SHELF NAMES ARE SNAPSHOTTED, AND WITHOUT THEM THE RECEIVING BRANCH
  ///   CANNOT READ ITS OWN DELIVERY NOTE. `inventory_locations` is branch-scoped
  ///   and its policy is absolute, so the sending branch's shelf is INVISIBLE to
  ///   the receiver — the join comes back NULL, not forbidden. That is the
  ///   isolation working correctly, and it leaves "where did this come from"
  ///   unanswerable at exactly the end that needs to ask it.
  ///
  ///   Weakening the location policy to fix it would open every branch's shelves
  ///   to every other branch to serve one screen. Copying the NAME onto the
  ///   document instead shares precisely the fact the document is about, and it
  ///   is also the more honest record: a shelf renamed or decommissioned next
  ///   year must not rewrite a delivery note from this one. Same reasoning and
  ///   the same shape as `invoices`' practitioner snapshot.
  ///
  ///   The ID stays as well, composite-FK'd, so the sending branch's own screens
  ///   still resolve a live row.
  fromLocationName String  @map("from_location_name") @db.VarChar(255)
  toLocationName   String? @map("to_location_name") @db.VarChar(255)

  status StockTransferStatus @default(DRAFT)

  notes String? @db.Text

  createdById        String    @map("created_by_id") @db.Uuid
  dispatchedAt       DateTime? @map("dispatched_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  dispatchedById     String?   @map("dispatched_by_id") @db.Uuid
  /// When the LAST line was received. A partially-received transfer has none.
  receivedAt         DateTime? @map("received_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  receivedById       String?   @map("received_by_id") @db.Uuid
  cancelledAt        DateTime? @map("cancelled_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  cancelledById      String?   @map("cancelled_by_id") @db.Uuid
  /// Why it was killed. CHECKed present whenever `cancelled_at` is — a transfer
  /// cancelled after dispatch has already moved stock twice and "why" is the
  /// only thing that makes the two compensating rows readable.
  cancellationReason String?   @map("cancellation_reason") @db.Text

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization Organization       @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /**
   * ⚠️ BOTH BRANCHES COMPOSE ON `(organization_id, id)`, AND THE LOCATIONS
   * COMPOSE ON THE BRANCH THEY BELONG TO — three columns, exactly as the
   * ledger's do. The two-column form would prove only that the shelf belongs
   * to this tenant, and a transfer FROM branch A citing a shelf at branch C is
   * precisely the row that makes stock leave a place it was never in.
   */
  fromBranch   Branch             @relation("TransferFromBranch", fields: [organizationId, fromBranchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  toBranch     Branch             @relation("TransferToBranch", fields: [organizationId, toBranchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  fromLocation InventoryLocation  @relation("TransferFromLocation", fields: [organizationId, fromBranchId, fromLocationId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  toLocation   InventoryLocation? @relation("TransferToLocation", fields: [organizationId, toBranchId, toLocationId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)

  createdBy    User  @relation("TransferCreatedBy", fields: [createdById], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  dispatchedBy User? @relation("TransferDispatchedBy", fields: [dispatchedById], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  receivedBy   User? @relation("TransferReceivedBy", fields: [receivedById], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  cancelledBy  User? @relation("TransferCancelledBy", fields: [cancelledById], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  lines StockTransferLine[]

  @@unique([organizationId, transferNumber])
  /// The composite-FK target the lines reference.
  @@unique([organizationId, id])
  /// "What is on its way to me" — the receiving branch's inbox, which is the
  /// screen this whole document exists to make possible.
  @@index([organizationId, toBranchId, status])
  /// "What have I sent" — the sending branch's outbox.
  @@index([organizationId, fromBranchId, status, createdAt(sort: Desc)])
  @@map("stock_transfers")
}

/// One product, one lot, one quantity, on one transfer.
///
/// ⚠️ `batch_id` AND `destination_batch_id` ARE TWO DIFFERENT LOTS OF THE SAME
///   PHYSICAL STOCK, AND THAT IS NOT A MODELLING MISTAKE. `batches` is
///   BRANCH-scoped — `(organization_id, branch_id, id)` is its composite key and
///   the whole file leans on it — so the lot row at the sending branch cannot be
///   cited by a movement at the receiving branch. Receipt therefore finds or
///   creates the branch's own row for the same manufacturer's lot number, expiry
///   and cost, and records it here so traceability crosses the gap: "which lot
///   did branch B receive, and where did it come from" is one join.
///
/// The alternative — making `batches` org-scoped so one row serves both sites —
/// was rejected in PI-2 and re-rejected here: a lot is received AT a site, at a
/// cost, on a date, and two sites receiving the same manufacturer's lot are two
/// receipts. See the `@@unique([organizationId, branchId, productId, lotNumber])`
/// on `batches` and its comment.
model StockTransferLine {
  id             String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId String @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid
  transferId     String @map("transfer_id") @db.Uuid

  productId String  @map("product_id") @db.Uuid
  /// The SENDING branch's lot. Required when the product is batch-tracked, which
  /// the ledger's tracking CHECK enforces on the leg rather than here.
  batchId   String? @map("batch_id") @db.Uuid
  serialId  String? @map("serial_id") @db.Uuid

  /// What the document says was sent, in the product's BASE UNIT. ⚠️ Not a
  /// quantity of stock — see the file header. Positive, CHECKed.
  sentQuantityBase     Decimal @map("sent_quantity_base") @db.Decimal(18, 6)
  /// What the receiver signed for. Starts at zero and only ever grows, and a
  /// CHECK stops it exceeding what was sent — over-receipt is a data-entry error
  /// on this document, not a discovery of stock, and it has its own adjustment.
  receivedQuantityBase Decimal @default(0) @map("received_quantity_base") @db.Decimal(18, 6)

  /// What the user actually typed, and in what — kept for the same reason the
  /// ledger keeps it: "2 boxes" must still read as "2 boxes" a year later.
  quantityEntered Decimal @map("quantity_entered") @db.Decimal(18, 6)
  unitId          String  @map("unit_id") @db.Uuid

  /// The receiving branch's own lot row, created at receipt. See the model
  /// comment.
  destinationBatchId String? @map("destination_batch_id") @db.Uuid

  // -- the lot's identity, snapshotted -----------------------------------------
  /// ⚠️ WITHOUT THESE THE RECEIVING BRANCH CANNOT CREATE ITS OWN LOT ROW, AND
  ///   THE WHOLE INTER-BRANCH FLOW STOPS AT RECEIPT. `batches` is branch-scoped
  ///   and the policy is absolute, so the SENDER'S lot is invisible to the
  ///   receiver — `findOrCreateDestinationBatch` reading it came back empty, not
  ///   forbidden. Receipt has to know the manufacturer's lot number and its
  ///   expiry to make the destination row mean the same physical stock, and it
  ///   can only learn them from the document.
  ///
  ///   Which is what a paper delivery note has always carried. Weakening the
  ///   batch policy instead would open every branch's lots, costs and expiry
  ///   dates to every other branch to serve one step of one flow.
  ///
  ///   ⚠️ THE COST TRAVELS TOO, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE RATHER THAN AN OVERSHARE.
  ///     Stock moved between two sites of ONE organization keeps its value — a
  ///     receiving branch valuing it at nothing would make every group-level
  ///     valuation wrong by the amount in transit. It is the same tenant's money
  ///     at both ends. `unit_cost_base` is in integer minor units per BASE unit,
  ///     as everywhere (PI-ADR-010).
  lotNumber      String?   @map("lot_number") @db.VarChar(128)
  manufacturedOn DateTime? @map("manufactured_on") @db.Date
  expiresOn      DateTime? @map("expires_on") @db.Date
  manufacturerId String?   @map("manufacturer_id") @db.Uuid
  unitCostBase   BigInt?   @map("unit_cost_base") @db.BigInt
  currency       String?   @db.Char(3)

  notes String? @db.Text

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization     Organization  @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  transfer         StockTransfer @relation(fields: [organizationId, transferId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// Plain FK into a possibly-platform row — `product_visible` policy, as on
  /// every other product reference in this file.
  product          Product       @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  unit             UnitOfMeasure @relation("TransferLineUnit", fields: [unitId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  /// Plain FK into a possibly-platform row, like every other manufacturer
  /// reference in the programme — a `manufacturer_visible` policy is the control.
  manufacturer     Manufacturer? @relation("TransferLineManufacturer", fields: [manufacturerId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  /**
   * ⚠️ THE TWO LOT REFERENCES ARE PLAIN `(organization_id, id)` COMPOSITES AND
   * NOT BRANCH-QUALIFIED ONES, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE CALL FROM THE LEDGER'S —
   * because they deliberately point at DIFFERENT branches. `batch_id` is at
   * `transfer.from_branch_id` and `destination_batch_id` is at
   * `transfer.to_branch_id`, and this table has neither column to compose on.
   * The service checks both, and the ledger legs are where the branch-qualified
   * form actually bites: a lot cited on the wrong branch's leg is unrepresentable
   * there, which is the place it would do harm.
   */
  batch            Batch?        @relation("TransferLineSourceBatch", fields: [organizationId, batchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  destinationBatch Batch?        @relation("TransferLineDestinationBatch", fields: [organizationId, destinationBatchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  serial           Serial?       @relation("TransferLineSerial", fields: [organizationId, serialId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// One line per (product, lot, serial) on a transfer. Sending the same lot
  /// twice on one document is a mistake with two plausible readings — did they
  /// mean the sum, or did they type it twice — and neither should be guessed.
  @@unique([organizationId, transferId, productId, batchId, serialId])
  @@index([organizationId, transferId])
  @@index([organizationId, productId])
  @@index([organizationId, batchId])
  @@map("stock_transfer_lines")
}

/// Stock spoken for and not yet gone (PI-3.4).
///
/// `RESERVED` becomes real here. It was a `StockStatus` member with no workflow
/// in PI-2, on purpose, so that this phase needed no enum migration.
///
/// ⚠️ THE ROW IS THE PAPERWORK; THE `RESERVATION` MOVEMENT IS THE FACT. Creating
///   one writes a `RESERVATION` movement (AVAILABLE → RESERVED) in the SAME
///   transaction, and releasing one writes a `RELEASE` (RESERVED → AVAILABLE).
///   A reservation row that committed without its movement would hold nothing
///   while claiming to, and two pharmacists would dispense the same last strip —
///   which is the entire failure this table exists to prevent.
///
/// ⚠️ `expires_at` IS NOT OPTIONAL PARANOIA. A reservation with no expiry that
///   nobody releases holds stock out of `AVAILABLE` for ever, and the clinic's
///   only visible symptom is that it cannot dispense a medicine it can see on
///   the shelf. The sweep in `apps/worker` releases them, one transaction per
///   reservation, for the same locking reason the expiry sweep does.
///
/// Branch-scoped: `branch_id` is NOT NULL and the policy is absolute, exactly as
/// for the ledger. Stock is reserved AT a place.
///
/// NO PHI. A reservation names a `reference_id` — which for PI-7 will be a
/// prescription and for PI-9 a procedure — and never a patient.
model StockReservation {
  id             String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid
  organizationId String @map("organization_id") @db.Uuid
  branchId       String @map("branch_id") @db.Uuid

  productId  String  @map("product_id") @db.Uuid
  batchId    String? @map("batch_id") @db.Uuid
  serialId   String? @map("serial_id") @db.Uuid
  locationId String  @map("location_id") @db.Uuid

  /// In the product's BASE UNIT, positive, CHECKed. This is how much sits in the
  /// `RESERVED` bucket because of this row — and the bucket, not this column, is
  /// what allocation reads.
  quantityBase Decimal @map("quantity_base") @db.Decimal(18, 6)

  status StockReservationStatus @default(ACTIVE)

  /// What it is being held FOR. The same non-FK pair as the ledger's, for the
  /// same reason: the domains that will fill it in do not exist yet.
  referenceType StockReferenceType @default(MANUAL) @map("reference_type")
  referenceId   String?            @map("reference_id") @db.Uuid

  /// When the sweep gives it back. See the model comment.
  expiresAt DateTime @map("expires_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  notes     String?  @db.Text

  createdById  String    @map("created_by_id") @db.Uuid
  /// Set together with a terminal `status` — CHECKed, because a released
  /// reservation with no release time is one nobody can put on a timeline.
  releasedAt   DateTime? @map("released_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  /// NULL when the SWEEP released it. That is how a screen tells "the pharmacist
  /// gave it back" from "it timed out", and the two mean different things.
  releasedById String?   @map("released_by_id") @db.Uuid

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at") @db.Timestamptz(6)

  organization Organization      @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  branch       Branch            @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId], references: [organizationId, id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// Plain FK into a possibly-platform row — `product_visible` policy.
  product      Product           @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  /// Branch-qualified, exactly as on the ledger: a reservation at branch A
  /// citing a lot or a shelf at branch B is unrepresentable.
  batch        Batch?            @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId, batchId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  serial       Serial?           @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId, serialId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)
  location     InventoryLocation @relation(fields: [organizationId, branchId, locationId], references: [organizationId, branchId, id], onDelete: Restrict)

  createdBy  User  @relation("ReservationCreatedBy", fields: [createdById], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  releasedBy User? @relation("ReservationReleasedBy", fields: [releasedById], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// The sweep's query: every ACTIVE reservation past its time. ⚠️ Deliberately
  /// NOT branch-qualified — the sweep asks the question across the whole tenant
  /// and then acts one branch at a time, exactly as the expiry sweep does.
  @@index([organizationId, status, expiresAt])
  /// "What is held at this shelf" — what allocation subtracts.
  @@index([organizationId, branchId, productId, status])
  /// "What is held for this prescription" — the release path, from PI-7.
  @@index([organizationId, referenceType, referenceId])
  @@map("stock_reservations")
}
